Here is a working podcast listening app's index.html:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>Podcast List</title>
  <style>
    body {
      font-family: sans-serif;
    }
    .episode {
      margin-bottom: 1rem;
      border: 1px solid #ccc;
      padding: 1rem;
      display: flex;
      justify-content: space-between;
      align-items: center;
    }
    .ethos-badge {
      margin-left: 0.5rem;
    }
    audio {
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 300px;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>Podcast List</h1>
  <input type="text" id="urlInput" placeholder="Enter a podcast URL">
  <button id="subscribeButton">Subscribe</button>
  <div id="episodes"></div>

  <script>
    const urlInput = document.getElementById('urlInput');
    const subscribeButton = document.getElementById('subscribeButton');
    const episodesContainer = document.getElementById('episodes');

    subscribeButton.addEventListener('click', async () => {
      const url = urlInput.value;
      urlInput.value = '';
      try {
        const response = await fetch(`/api/subscribe?url=${encodeURIComponent(url)}`);
        if (!response.ok) {
          throw new Error(`Fetch error: ${response.status}`);
        }
        const data = await response.json();
        episodesContainer.innerHTML = ''; // Clear previous episodes
        data.episodes.forEach(episode => {
          const episodeElement = document.createElement('div');
          episodeElement.className = 'episode';
          episodeElement.innerHTML = `
            <h2>${episode.title}</h2>
            <p>Published: ${episode.pubDate}</p>
            <div class="ethos-badge">
              <span style="color: green; font-size: 1.2rem;">${episode.ethos === 'pass' ? 'Pass' : (episode.ethos === 'conflict' ? 'Conflict' : 'No Signal')}</span>
            </div>
            <audio controls src="${episode.audioUrl}" preload="auto"></audio>
          `;
          episodesContainer.appendChild(episodeElement);
        });
      } catch (error) {
        episodesContainer.innerHTML = `<p>Error fetching episodes: ${error.message}</p>`;
      }
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

It already satisfies this contract, which you must keep satisfying:
The backend already runs at the same origin as this page (relative URLs). It exposes:

  GET /api/subscribe?url=<feed-url>
    Fetches and parses that podcast's real RSS feed. Returns JSON:
    { show: { title }, episodes: [ { title, pubDate, description, audioUrl, ethos, logosFindingCount } ] }
    "ethos" is one of "pass" | "conflict" | "no_signal" (a real charter check already run server-side).

  GET /api/episodes?show=<show-title>
    Returns the same episodes array for a show already subscribed to (no re-fetch).

Write ONE self-contained index.html file (inline <style> and <script>, no external libraries, no build step) for a podcast LISTENING app:
- A text input + "Subscribe" button that calls /api/subscribe?url=... (URL-encode the value) and renders the returned episode list.
- Each episode shows its title, publish date, and an ethos badge (green if "pass"/"no_signal", red if "conflict").
- Each episode has a real HTML5 <audio controls> element whose src is that episode's own audioUrl, so clicking play actually streams and plays the real audio file.
- Use fetch() and plain DOM APIs only (no frameworks). Handle a fetch error by showing a message, never by throwing unhandled.
- Return ONLY the HTML file's contents in one fenced code block, nothing else.

Now: emulate Spotify's visual aesthetic (dark theme, bold green accent, bold sans-serif typography) — specifically for the relationship between a single episode playing and the list it came from: a persistent now-playing bar at the bottom for whichever episode is active, while the list above stays browsable and highlights which row is currently playing. Keep every API call and the <audio> playback working exactly as before — improve the layout, styling, and interaction, not the data contract. Return the WHOLE improved file in one fenced code block, nothing else.

